+      "source_fragment": "82-0c6b3dc40877::printed-unit:15::source:14:0",
+      "page": 14,
+      "block_index": 0,
+      "original_source_text": "the joint planning is for coordination between the City and affected Service Providers in order to\ndetermine the best, most efficient, most aesthetic and least obtrusive use of the Right-of-way,\nconsistent with safety, and to minimize traffic and other disruptions, including Street cuts. As\nnecessary, the Service Provider shall furnish maps showing the location of Facilities of the\nService Provider within the project boundaries. (See OPMC 13.12.350 & 13.12.360.)\nAs soon as working drawings are available for the relocation/improvements, the Service\nProvider shall provide the City with written notice and the anticipated bid letting date of said\nrelocation/improvement. It is anticipated that such notice should occur at least (180) days in\nadvance, if not more. The City shall respond with any conflicts with the work or proposed\nconstruction schedule within (30) days of receipt of the working drawings.\nFollowing notice by the City in the form of the approval of a Right-of-way permit for\nrelocation/improvements, the Service Provider will provide the City with timely notice of any\nrelocation and/or improvements delay and the reasons therefore.\nIt is the intent of this section for both the City and the Service Provider to cooperate with one\nanother so that the need for the Facility relocation/improvements initiated by the Service\nProvider is minimized and, when required and feasible, these relocations/improvements will be\ncoordinated with ongoing Public Improvement projects and other ROW-user projects.",
